[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,8.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(5.5,0);
\foreach \v/\p in {4000/0,4100/1,4200/2,4300/3,4400/4,4500/5,4600/6,4700/7} {\draw (-0.2,\p)--(0,\p); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\p) {$\v$};}
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (0.6,0) rectangle (1.6,3.0);
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (2.0,0) rectangle (3.0,6.5);
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (3.4,0) rectangle (4.4,2.0);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.1,0) {Mon};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (2.5,0) {Tue};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3.9,0) {Wed};
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-1.9,3.5) {Visitors};
\node[below,font=\small] at (2.50,-1.0) {Day};
\node[font=\small] at (2.5,8.1) {Daily website visitors};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Why does this graph make Tuesday's visitors look far greater than Monday's?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,5.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4.8,0);
\foreach \v/\p in {100/1,200/2,300/3,400/4} {\draw (-0.2,\p)--(0,\p); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\p) {$\v$};}
\node[left,font=\small] at (-0.2,0) {$0$};
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (0.6,0) rectangle (1.2,3.0);
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (2.0,0) rectangle (3.6,3.3);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (0.9,0) {Author A};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (2.8,0) {Author B};
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-1.3,2.7) {Books sold};
\node[below,font=\small] at (1.85,-1.0) {Author};
\node[font=\small] at (2.2,5.1) {Books sold at the fair};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Author A sold $300$ books and Author B sold $330$.
What makes this graph misleading?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\fill[orange!40,draw=black] (0,0) rectangle (0.8,0.8);
\fill[orange!40,draw=black] (2.2,0) rectangle (3.8,1.6);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (0.4,0) {2020};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3.0,0) {2021};
\node[above,font=\small] at (0.4,0.8) {$100$};
\node[above,font=\small] at (3.0,1.6) {$200$};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.7,2.6) {Students achieving Band 6};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The number of students achieving Band 6 doubled from $100$ to $200$.
Why does the pictograph overstate the increase?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,4.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(5.2,0);
\foreach \v/\p in {100/1,200/2,300/3} {\draw (-0.2,\p)--(0,\p); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\p) {$\v$};}
\node[left,font=\small] at (-0.2,0) {$0$};
\foreach \x/\lbl in {1/2016,2.5/2017,4/2022} {\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,0) {\lbl};}
\draw[thick,blue] (1,1)--(2.5,2)--(4,3);
\fill[blue] (1,1) circle (2pt) (2.5,2) circle (2pt) (4,3) circle (2pt);
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.9,1.5) {Members};
\node[below,font=\small] at (2.50,-1.0) {Year};
\node[font=\small] at (2.5,4.1) {Gym membership};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Why does this line graph make the membership look like it grew at a steady rate?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,4.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(5.2,0);
\foreach \v/\p in {20/1,40/2,60/3} {\draw (-0.2,\p)--(0,\p); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\p) {$\v\%$};}
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (0.6,0) rectangle (1.6,3.0);
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (2.0,0) rectangle (3.0,2.75);
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (3.4,0) rectangle (4.4,2.25);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.1,0) {Maths};
\node[below,font=\tiny] at (2.5,0) {English};
\node[below,font=\tiny] at (3.9,0) {Science};
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-2.0,1.5) {Share of A grades};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (2.50,-1.0) {Subject};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (2.5,4.1) {Share of the school's A grades};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph claims to show each subject's share of the school's A grades ($60\%$, $55\%$, $45\%$).
Why is it misleading?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,7.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(5.5,0);
\foreach \v/\p in {0/0,50/1,100/2,150/3,200/4,250/5,300/6} {\draw (-0.2,\p)--(0,\p); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\p) {$\v$};}
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (0.6,0) rectangle (1.6,4.8);
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (2.0,0) rectangle (3.0,5.2);
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (3.4,0) rectangle (4.4,4.6);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.1,0) {School X};
\node[below,font=\tiny] at (2.5,0) {School Y};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3.9,0) {School Z};
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-1.9,3) {Trees planted};
\node[below,font=\small] at (2.50,-1.0) {School};
\node[font=\small] at (2.5,7.1) {Trees planted by each school};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Is this graph of trees planted misleading?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.65);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4.8,0);
\foreach \v/\p in {20/0,25/0.75,30/1.5,35/2.25,40/3.0} {\draw (-0.2,\p)--(0,\p); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\p) {$\v$};}
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (0.6,0) rectangle (1.2,1.5);
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (2.2,0) rectangle (4.0,2.1);
\node[below,font=\tiny] at (0.9,0) {Stall A};
\node[below,font=\tiny] at (3.1,0) {Stall B};
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-1.0,1.1) {Profit (\$)};
\node[below,font=\small] at (2.00,-1.0) {Stall};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (2.3,3.35) {Weekly profit by stall};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Stall A made $\$30$ and Stall B made $\$34$.
Which pair of features makes this graph misleading?
